ion to this particular  age this particular phase this  particular sex yeah that's a good  question so we like we like to look at  through age 24 or so because that's when  the brain really kind of solidifies and  our frontal lobes stop developing and  we're adults at age 24. and we know that  the onset of mental health problems 50  of disorders starts before the age of  14. before 14 and 75 before the age of  24. so that's really what we want to  look at is that sort of childhood  through early adulthood what's happening  before age 14 that would cause you  mental anguish and stress i think it's a  complicated answer and it's something we  call a stress diathesis model which  basically means we have a biological  predisposition and we have the  environment we grow up in and if we're  sort of born prone to a mental health  disorder it's going to come out earlier  rather than later in life so everybody  wants to raise resilient kids right it's  a goal as a parent you don't want to  coddle your kids but it's hard to know  where the line is between telling a kid  to shake it off and get back in there  and it being a chronic problem a real  problem right so you want to monitor  it's okay for kids to have setbacks and  we want kids to have moments of failure  that builds character it builds  resilience so we don't want to spare  children from any disappointments  necessarily but we want a monitor to  make sure that they're functioning right  so if it goes on for say two weeks of a  child being consistently sad and down  and not interested in things they used  to enjoy then we might think oh this is  starting to seem like a depressive  episode and not just a momentary setback  something i had to learn as a parent was  each kid receives information  differently yeah so i have to parent  them differently i can't have the same  mental health conversation with my 18  year old as i do in my 16 year old as i  do with my daughter who's 12. um what  advice do you give to parents 